  "scope": "Deterministic accepted simulation measurements. These are not human playability, reaction-time, browser paint-latency, contrast-perception, or hardware performance measurements. No live profile saves or awards are involved.",
  "method": {
    "cases": "18 current winning proofs: Quiet Return (local with runner), Quiet Channel (broadcast with runner), and Field Links (local specialists/cable), each Solo/Team at Slow/Normal/Fast. Each uses its accepted level seed 17 and hazard seed 17; no inference about other hazard seeds.",
    "authority": "Every current and archived comparison replay is stepped using only its accepted turn journal, and the exported complete replay is checked for deep equality with its recorded proof.",
    "interval": "A move interval uses the displayed pre-step state, held from its elapsedMs boundary until the next accepted boundary. Exposure milliseconds sum those actual simulation intervals when classicSignalStrength is positive.",
    "protectedArea": "Union of all living heads’ inclusive two-cell squares, with wrap distances, plus source cells; matches renderer protection. Cable counts unique non-head cells outside that union.",
    "ordinaryTargetKinds": [
      "still",
      "runner",
      "patroller",
      "sprinter",
      "courier",
      "refuge",
      "switchback",
      "pair",
      "shield",
      "brace",
      "perimeter",
      "contour",
      "rover",
      "ricochet"
    ],
    "ordinaryTargetDefinition": "Counts non-specialist prey kinds listed above. Jammer, lane, relay, eroder, guard, relay pads, projectiles and pickups are excluded.",
    "cellSamples": "cellMoveSamples sums the number of distant cells across exposed intervals; repeated cells across time are repeated samples. cellMilliseconds is the corresponding exposure-time integral, not a unique enemy/cable count.",
    "responses": "Zero extra moves/ms means the projected receiver is clear at the same accepted boundary that exits coverage or catches the transmitting source; it is not a wall-clock or pixel-display latency claim.",
    "absence": "Zero exposure and empty response samples remain explicit. They do not prove that every possible route or hazard seed avoids interference."
  },
